-- Passage rg8-figurative-language-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5aeb9ff3-6716-500d-8fb7-b2db9eb52faf', 'rg8-figurative-language-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-figurative-language'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Smallest Plot$q$, $q$Darnell's plot in the Maple Avenue Community Garden was the smallest one on the lot, and it looked even smaller next to Mrs. Petrosyan's. Her tomato vines climbed their cages like eager kids on a jungle gym. His single pepper plant stood alone, short and pale.

"Your pepper is slender," Mrs. Petrosyan said kindly when she saw him frowning at it.

"It's skinny," Darnell corrected her. "Let's be honest."

She laughed and handed him a bucket of compost. "Plants are like people. Feed them well, and they grow."

All summer, Darnell came every evening after practice. He watered, pulled weeds, and spread compost around the stem. His mother called him stubborn. Mrs. Petrosyan called him determined. Darnell decided he liked her word better.

By August, the pepper plant had doubled in height. Six green peppers hung from its branches like small lanterns. Darnell picked the biggest one and carried it to Mrs. Petrosyan's plot as if it were a gold medal.

"For you," he said. "Payment for the compost."

She turned it over in her hands. "This," she said, "is the most expensive pepper I have ever received. It cost you a whole summer."

Darnell grinned. He knew exactly what she meant.$q$, 'draft')
