   $q$Some residents called the tower "outdated," and others called it "historic." How do these two words differ?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Both mean old, but \"historic\" sounds useless and \"outdated\" sounds important.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reversed-connotation"},{"text":"They mean the same thing and carry the same feeling, so either works.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misses-connotation"},{"text":"\"Outdated\" means old, while \"historic\" means newly built to look old.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-denotation"},{"text":"Both mean old, but \"outdated\" sounds useless and \"historic\" sounds important.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Both mean old, but "outdated" sounds useless and "historic" sounds important.$q$, $q$The residents who said "outdated" wanted the tower torn down, while those who said "historic" wanted to save it, which shows the opposite connotations. The words share the meaning "old," so the difference is in feeling, not facts.$q$, 'draft', 'fdde9a57-60d5-59d2-92d7-776db8e6d3c5')
